"""English — details, faith_b_2 (40 questions)."""

D = {
"faith_111": "The call comes in Surah At-Tahrim: \"O you who have believed, repent to Allah with sincere repentance, so that your Lord may remove from you your misdeeds and admit you into gardens beneath which rivers flow\" (66:8). The same verse names the Day when Allah will not disgrace the Prophet ﷺ and those who believed with him, their light running before them. It follows the command to protect oneself and one's family from the Fire (66:6). A Muslim learns that repentance is a whole turning back, and that its fruit is forgiveness and light.",
"faith_112": "The verse belongs to Surah Ash-Shura: \"And it is He who accepts repentance from His servants and pardons misdeeds, and He knows what you do\" (42:25). The next verse adds that He answers those who believe and do righteous deeds and increases them from His bounty (42:26). Acceptance, pardon and full knowledge of what people do are joined in one sentence: the One who forgives is the One who sees everything. A Muslim learns that nothing about him is hidden from Allah, and that this is why he turns back to Him with hope.",
"faith_113": "The verse continues: \"and who can forgive sins except Allah? And they do not persist in what they have done while they know\" (3:135). The verse before describes the God-fearing who spend in ease and hardship, restrain their anger and pardon people (3:134); the verse after names the reward, forgiveness and gardens beneath which rivers flow (3:136). The praised people are not those who never slip, but those who, having slipped, remember Allah at once. A Muslim learns that turning back quickly is itself a mark of the righteous.",
"faith_114": "The two verses read: \"O you who have believed, remember Allah with much remembrance, and exalt Him morning and evening\" (33:41-42). The next verse gives the reason: \"It is He who sends blessings upon you, and His angels, that He may bring you out from darkness into the light\" (33:43). Earlier in the surah, the men and women who remember Allah much are promised forgiveness and a great reward (33:35). A Muslim keeps Allah on his tongue and in his heart through the day, and marks its two ends with glorification.",
"faith_115": "The passage opens with the sign itself: \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the night and the day are signs for those of understanding\" (3:190). Their reflection ends in a prayer: \"Our Lord, You did not create this aimlessly; exalted are You; so protect us from the punishment of the Fire\" (3:191). Their Lord answers that He will never let the work of any of them be lost (3:195). A Muslim learns to look at the sky and the turning of day and night as a reason to remember Allah and to pray.",
"faith_116": "The command is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ: \"So by mercy from Allah you were lenient with them; had you been harsh and hard of heart, they would have left you. So pardon them, ask forgiveness for them and consult them in the matter\" (3:159). The next verse completes it: \"If Allah helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who can help you after Him?\" (3:160). A Muslim learns the order: gentleness, consultation, a firm decision, then trust in Allah rather than in his own plan.",
"faith_117": "Surah Ash-Sharh is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ and opens with a question: \"Did We not expand for you your breast, and remove from you your burden which weighed upon your back, and raise high for you your renown?\" (94:1-4). Then comes the promise, said twice: \"Indeed, with hardship comes ease\" (94:5-6). The surah closes with the command to stand up when finished and turn one's longing to the Lord (94:7-8). A Muslim learns that one task done is the doorway to the next act of devotion, and that his hope is kept for his Lord.",
"faith_118": "The verse names five things the worldly life is made of before the parable: \"amusement and diversion and adornment and boasting among yourselves and rivalry in wealth and children\" (57:20). Then it sets the Hereafter beside it, \"severe punishment, and forgiveness from Allah and approval,\" and ends: \"and what is the worldly life except the enjoyment of delusion\" (57:20). The next verse says what to do instead: race toward forgiveness and a Garden as wide as the heavens and the earth (57:21). A Muslim learns to use this world without being deceived by it.",
"faith_119": "The verse ends with a second comparison: the enduring good deeds are \"better with your Lord for reward and better for hope\" (18:46). It comes right after a parable: \"the life of this world is like rain We send down from the sky; the vegetation of the earth mingles with it, then it becomes dry stubble scattered by the winds\" (18:45). Before that, Surah Al-Kahf tells of the man with two gardens who boasted of his wealth (18:34) and lost everything. A Muslim learns to value what stays over what fades, and to place his hope in deeds.",
"faith_120": "The question is asked near the end of Surah Al-Mu'minun: \"Then did you think that We created you aimlessly and that to Us you would not be returned?\" (23:115). Just before it, those who wasted their lives are asked how long they stayed on earth, and answer, \"a day or part of a day\" (23:113). Right after it comes the answer: \"So exalted is Allah, the Sovereign, the Truth; there is no deity except Him\" (23:116). A Muslim learns that a life with a purpose is a life that expects to return to Allah.",
"faith_121": "The verse reads: \"So remember Me; I will remember you. And be grateful to Me and do not be ungrateful\" (2:152). It follows the verses on the change of the qiblah and the favour of a Messenger sent from among the believers, \"reciting to you Our verses, purifying you, and teaching you the Book and wisdom\" (2:151). The next verse tells them to seek help through patience and prayer, for Allah is with the patient (2:153). A Muslim learns that his remembrance of Allah is met by Allah's remembrance of him, and that thanks is the fitting reply to guidance.",
"faith_122": "The verse opens with the command: \"O you who have believed, obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you\" (4:59). Then comes the rule for disagreement, referring it to Allah and the Messenger ﷺ, and the verse ends: \"That is best and finest in outcome\" (4:59). The verse before it commands rendering trusts to their owners and judging between people with justice (4:58). A Muslim learns that when a dispute arises, the Qur'an and the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ are where he looks for the answer.",
"faith_123": "The verse continues: \"and upon their Lord they rely\" (8:2). The following verses complete the portrait: \"who establish prayer and spend from what We have provided them; those are the believers in truth; for them are degrees with their Lord, and forgiveness, and noble provision\" (8:3-4). The surah opens with the question about the spoils of war and the command to set right what is between the believers (8:1), so this description comes as its answer. A Muslim learns that faith is felt in the heart and seen in trust, prayer and giving.",
"faith_124": "The verse is a declaration the Prophet ﷺ is commanded to make, and it continues: \"No partner has He; and this I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims\" (6:162-163). Just before, he is told to say that his Lord guided him to a straight path, \"the religion of Ibrahim, inclining toward truth\" (6:161). The verse gathers prayer, sacrifice, living and dying into one direction, so that nothing is left outside the worship of Allah. A Muslim learns to intend his whole life, not only his prayers, for the Lord of the worlds.",
"faith_125": "The verse continues: \"inclining to truth, and to establish prayer and give zakat; and that is the correct religion\" (98:5). Surah Al-Bayyinah is speaking of the People of the Scripture, who divided only after clear evidence had come to them (98:4), a Messenger reciting purified pages (98:2). They had been commanded nothing else: sincere worship, prayer and zakat. The surah then calls those who believe and do righteous deeds \"the best of creatures\" (98:7). A Muslim learns that worship counts when it is meant purely for Allah, with prayer and zakat as its visible shape.",
"faith_126": "Surah Muhammad describes the garden in detail: \"in it are rivers of water unaltered, rivers of milk whose taste never changes, rivers of wine delicious to those who drink, and rivers of purified honey\" (47:15). The same verse adds every kind of fruit and forgiveness from their Lord, then asks whether such people are like those who abide in the Fire and drink scalding water (47:15). Four rivers are named, each one unlike anything the earth offers. A Muslim learns that the Garden is described in things he can picture, so that he may long for it.",
"faith_127": "The verse belongs to a portrait of the believers in Surah As-Sajdah. They fall down in prostration when reminded of the verses (32:15); \"their sides part from their beds, they call upon their Lord in fear and hope, and from what We have provided them they spend\" (32:16). Then comes the hidden reward: \"No soul knows what has been hidden for them of comfort for the eyes\" (32:17). The verse after says that a believer and one who defiantly disobeys are not equal (32:18). A Muslim learns that the night prayers no one sees are answered with a reward no one can imagine.",
"faith_128": "The verse reads in full: \"For those who have done good is the best reward and more; no darkness will cover their faces, nor humiliation. Those are the companions of Paradise; they will abide in it forever\" (10:26). The verse before it is an invitation: \"And Allah invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to a straight path\" (10:25). The verse after sets the contrast: an evil deed is repaid only with its like (10:27). A Muslim learns that Allah's reward for good is never merely equal to the deed, but greater.",
"faith_129": "The verse describes the arrival: \"Those who feared their Lord will be driven to Paradise in groups until, when they reach it and its gates are opened, its keepers say to them: Peace be upon you; you have become pure, so enter it to abide eternally\" (39:73). Their answer follows: \"Praise be to Allah, who has fulfilled His promise to us\" (39:74). Two verses earlier, another group is driven to Hell, and its keepers question them (39:71). A Muslim learns that the greeting of peace he gives on earth is the greeting waiting at the gate of the Garden.",
"faith_130": "The call in 3:133 is to hasten to two things at once: a forgiveness from the Lord, and a Garden as wide as the heavens and the earth, made ready for the God-fearing. The verses that follow say who these are: \"who spend in ease and in hardship, who restrain anger and pardon people; and Allah loves the doers of good\" (3:134), and who, when they wrong themselves, remember Allah and ask forgiveness (3:135). Their reward is forgiveness and gardens beneath which rivers flow (3:136). A Muslim learns that hastening to forgiveness means giving, holding back anger and pardoning.",
"faith_131": "The verse goes on to describe that Fire: \"over it are angels, stern and severe, who do not disobey Allah in what He commands them, but do what they are commanded\" (66:6). Two verses later the surah calls the believers to sincere repentance (66:8). Near its end it gives the wife of Nuh and the wife of Lut, who were under two righteous servants of Allah and betrayed them, and the wife of Fir'awn, who prayed for a house near Allah (66:10-11). A Muslim learns that guarding himself is not enough: his family is a trust he must also keep from the Fire.",
"faith_132": "The verse continues past belief to giving: wealth given \"despite love for it, to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveller, those who ask, and for freeing slaves\" (2:177). Then come establishing prayer, giving zakat, keeping promises, and patience \"in poverty and hardship and in time of battle\" (2:177). The verse ends: \"Those are the ones who have been true, and those are the righteous\" (2:177). It comes after the verses on the direction of prayer, and says what facing a direction is for. A Muslim learns that righteousness is faith joined to open hands and a patient heart.",
"faith_133": "The verse continues: \"and you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, and He will inform you of what you used to do\" (9:105). It follows the verses about those who confessed their sins, having mixed a good deed with a bad one (9:102), and the command to take charity from their wealth to purify them (9:103). Deeds are not done in secret: Allah, His Messenger ﷺ and the believers see them, and every one is brought back. A Muslim learns to work well, since his work is seen and will be shown to him.",
"faith_134": "The surah has four verses in all: \"Say: He is Allah, One; Allah, the Eternal Refuge; He neither begets nor is born; and there is none comparable to Him\" (112:1-4). It opens with the command \"Say\", so the Prophet ﷺ is told to announce these words, and it moves from oneness, to the denial of any child or parent, to the denial of any equal. The final verse (112:4) closes every door through which likeness could enter. A Muslim learns to think of Allah as unlike anything he knows, and recites this surah as the plainest statement of his belief.",
"faith_135": "The verse reads: \"He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden, and He is Knowing of all things\" (57:3). It comes at the head of Surah Al-Hadid, after the statement that whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allah (57:1) and that His is the dominion, giving life and causing death (57:2). The next verse adds that He knows what enters the earth and what comes out of it, and \"He is with you wherever you are\" (57:4). A Muslim learns that Allah is before every beginning and after every end, and that His knowledge covers all.",
"faith_136": "The names continue in the same verse: \"the Exalted in Might, the Compeller, the Supreme; exalted is Allah above whatever they associate with Him\" (59:23). The verse before opened the list by denying any other deity and naming Him Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful (59:22); the verse after adds the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner, \"to Him belong the best names\" (59:24). Three verses gather more than a dozen names at the close of Surah Al-Hashr. A Muslim learns these names as a way of knowing Allah.",
"faith_137": "The word shahadah means testimony: the one who says it bears witness to two things, that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad ﷺ is His Messenger. Both halves are found in the Qur'an, which says, \"Know that there is no deity except Allah\" (47:19) and \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah\" (48:29). A reader finds the formula in the adhan called from every mosque and in the tashahhud of every prayer. A Muslim learns that Islam begins with this testimony and that everything else he does rests upon it.",
"faith_138": "Takbir means \"making great\": to say Allahu akbar is to declare that Allah is greater than everything. The Qur'an commands it with the same root, \"and magnify Him with great magnification\" (17:111), and says the days of Ramadan are completed \"so that you may magnify Allah for having guided you\" (2:185). A reader hears the takbir at the opening of the call to prayer, at the start of every prayer, and at each movement within it. A Muslim learns that takbir, tasbih, tahmid and tahlil are the plain words of his remembrance.",
"faith_139": "The name basmalah is formed from the opening words of the formula itself, bismi-llah, \"in the name of Allah\". A reader finds it at the head of every surah except the ninth, At-Tawbah, and once inside a surah, in the letter Sulayman sent to the queen of Saba: \"It is from Sulayman, and it is: In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful\" (27:30). The formula names Allah by two names of mercy before anything else is said. A Muslim opens his recitation, his meal and his work with it.",
"faith_140": "The words, inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi raji'un, are given in 2:156 as the mark of the patient. The verse comes in the middle of a promise of trial: \"We will surely test you with something of fear and hunger and loss of wealth, lives and fruits; but give good tidings to the patient\" (2:155). The reward is named at once: blessings from their Lord, and mercy, and they are the guided (2:157). The sentence makes two statements: that we are Allah's, and that we go back to Him. A Muslim learns to answer loss with these words.",
"faith_141": "The instruction continues: \"And remember your Lord when you forget, and say: It may be that my Lord will guide me to something nearer than this to right guidance\" (18:23-24). It comes in Surah Al-Kahf straight after the account of the sleepers of the cave, whose number people disputed, and the Prophet ﷺ was told to say, \"My Lord knows best their number\" (18:22). The small word for tomorrow carries the same lesson: what is to come is known to Allah alone. A Muslim learns to say in sha' Allah when he speaks of the future.",
"faith_142": "The verse is the last of Surah Luqman, and it ends: \"Indeed, Allah is Knowing, Acquainted\" (31:34). The verse before it warns of a Day when no father will avail his son nor a son his father, and tells people not to let the life of this world deceive them (31:33). Five things are named in 31:34 that no creature can know in advance: the Hour, the rain, the wombs, tomorrow's earnings and the place of death. A Muslim learns to leave the unseen to Allah and to prepare for a Day whose time only He knows.",
"faith_143": "The verse reads: \"For every nation there is a term; so when their term has come, they will not remain behind an hour, nor will they go ahead\" (7:34). It follows the verse in which Allah forbids immoralities, sin, oppression and speaking about Allah without knowledge (7:33), and leads into the address to the children of Adam: whoever fears Allah and does right will have no fear nor grief (7:35). The Qur'an repeats the sentence in Surah Yunus (10:49). A Muslim learns that the end of every people is fixed, and that the time before it is the time to reform.",
"faith_144": "The verse completes the promise: \"and We will surely give them their reward according to the best of what they used to do\" (16:97). The verse before it explains why: \"Whatever you have will run out, but what is with Allah is lasting\" (16:96). The verse after turns to the recitation of the Qur'an, telling the reader to seek refuge in Allah from Shaytan (16:98). Two rewards are promised, a good life here and a reward measured by one's best deeds, to men and women alike. A Muslim learns that faith with good work is repaid in both worlds.",
"faith_145": "The verse continues: \"And if you obey Allah and His Messenger, He will not deprive you of anything of your deeds; indeed, Allah is Forgiving and Merciful\" (49:14). The next verse says who the believers are: those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, then do not doubt, and strive with their wealth and their lives (49:15). Later the bedouins are told not to count their Islam as a favour to the Prophet ﷺ, since Allah favoured them by guiding them to faith (49:17). A Muslim learns that saying the words of Islam is a beginning, and that faith settles in the heart through obedience.",
"faith_146": "The rule is decreed after a killing: Surah Al-Ma'idah has just told of the two sons of Adam, one of whom, his offering unaccepted, killed his brother and was left among the regretful (5:27-31). The verse opens, \"Because of that, We decreed upon the Children of Israel\" (5:32), and lays down that one life killed weighs like all mankind and one life saved weighs the same. It closes by noting that messengers came with clear proofs and that many still transgressed (5:32). A Muslim learns to see every single human life as carrying the weight of all humanity.",
"faith_147": "The verse ends: \"and they will not be wronged\" (6:160). It comes at the close of Surah Al-An'am, after the warning that the Prophet ﷺ has nothing to do with those who split their religion into sects (6:159), and before his declaration that his prayer, sacrifice, living and dying are for Allah (6:162). Surah Al-Baqarah goes further for spending in Allah's way, likening it to a grain that grows seven ears with a hundred grains in each (2:261). A Muslim learns that good is counted generously and evil only by its measure, so no good deed is small.",
"faith_148": "The verse reads: \"O mankind, you are the ones in need of Allah, while Allah is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy\" (35:15). The next verses add: \"If He wills, He can remove you and bring a new creation, and that is not difficult for Allah\" (35:16-17). Just before, the surah says of the false gods that they do not own so much as the membrane of a date-stone (35:13), and that if called upon they do not hear (35:14). A Muslim learns that his worship adds nothing to Allah and everything to himself, and he asks from the only One who lacks nothing.",
"faith_149": "Surah Al-'Asr is three verses long, and the quotation is the whole of it: \"By time, indeed mankind is in loss, except those who believe and do righteous deeds and enjoin one another to the truth and enjoin one another to patience\" (103:1-3). It opens with an oath by time itself, then states the rule for everyone, then gives the single exception. Surah Al-Balad names the same pairing: \"those who believed and enjoined one another to patience and enjoined one another to compassion\" (90:17). A Muslim learns that faith and good deeds are held together by people who remind each other.",
"faith_150": "The verse goes on: \"We make no distinction between any of His messengers. And they say: We hear and we obey; Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the return\" (2:285). It is the first of the two verses that close Surah Al-Baqarah; the second says that Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity and teaches the prayer, \"Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err\" (2:286). Four objects of belief are named, and the messengers are accepted whole, with none left out. A Muslim learns to say these words as his own: to hear, to obey, and to ask forgiveness.",
}
